    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-158198",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9e4fd256-a726-523c-84dc-f62d4e2711cc",
      "knowledge_point": "Sketching the Domain of a Multivariable Function",
      "problem": "Which of the following shaded regions corresponds to the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}?$",
      "worked_solution": "The function is well-defined at all points $(x,y)$ for which the following condition is satisfied:\n\n- The expression under the square root is non-negative.\n\nAs a result, the points in the domain must satisfy the following inequalities:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,{y}^{2}\\le {x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-|x|\\le y\\le |x|\\}.\n$$\n\nSince the inequality involves the $\\le $ sign, the curves are part of the domain. Therefore, we draw the solid curves $y=-|x|$ and $y=|x|,$ and shade the area *below* the curve $y=|x|,$ and *above* the curve $y=-|x|.$\n\nThe resulting domain is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14098447/assets/95f187d2857973e4fea83eedf47bd54c153ce2a77f278cfb992b222de2d91ab8.png)",
